====================================================
 Step 5: Plurigenera & Kodaira dimension
====================================================

W is birational to S × P^1 where S: Y^2 = X(X+1)(X+q^2), parameter q.

Kodaira dimension is birational invariant; product formula:
  κ(S × P^1) = κ(S) + κ(P^1) = κ(S) + (-∞) = -∞

Plurigenera P_n(S × P^1) = h^0(n K_{S×P^1})
  = h^0(S, n K_S) * h^0(P^1, n K_{P^1})
  = h^0(S, n K_S) * h^0(P^1, O(-2n))
For n >= 1, h^0(P^1, O(-2n)) = 0.

=> P_n(W) = 0 for all n >= 1.
=> h^0(K_W) = 0 — W is NOT of general type.

=== Kodaira dimension of S (the elliptic surface) ===

S = E_total -> A^1_q is the universal E_PCP(q).
Singular fibers occur at q = 0, q = ±∞, q = ±1, q = ±i (places where E_PCP degenerates).

Equation: Y^2 = X^3 + (1+q^2) X^2 + q^2 X.
Discriminant: Δ(q) = 16 q^4 (1 - q^2)^2 (up to constant).

Disc of X(X+1)(X+q^2) in X: q^8 - 2*q^6 + q^4

Singular fibers of S -> P^1_q:
  q=0: cubic X^2(X+1), nodal, type I_2 or similar
  q=±1: cubic X(X+1)^2, nodal
  q=∞: similar by change of variable

All fibers have multiplicative reduction (nodal).
Sum of Euler numbers of singular fibers = 12 χ(O_S) for elliptic K3, etc.

Detailed Kodaira-Neron analysis requires care at q=±i and q=∞.
For our purposes: S is a RATIONAL elliptic surface or a K3 (κ ∈ {-∞, 0}).

j-invariant (Legendre with λ = q^2):
  j(E_PCP(q)) = 256 (q^4 - q^2 + 1)^3 / [q^4 (1 - q^2)^2]

Non-constant in q => S is non-isotrivial.

Degree of j as map P^1_q -> P^1_j: numerator deg 12, denominator deg 8.
Degree = max(12, 8) = 12 (numerator has higher degree).

For elliptic surface with section: deg(j) = 12 χ(O_S).
So χ(O_S) = 1 => S is a RATIONAL elliptic surface OR a K3 (if simply connected).

With χ(O_S) = 1 and section present, S is a rational elliptic surface (κ = -∞)
UNLESS h^1(O_S) = 0 and we have additional structure pushing to K3.

Since the j-map has degree 12 and the base is P^1_q,
S is a RATIONAL elliptic surface (κ(S) = -∞).

=> W birational to S × P^1 with S rational => W is birational to a rational variety!

=== W IS RATIONAL ===

This strengthens the conclusion: W has the maximal possible W(Q) — Zariski-dense rational points.
PCP closure CANNOT come from global geometry of W.

Summary:
  - W is uniruled (in fact rational).
  - κ(W) = -∞.
  - P_n(W) = 0 for all n >= 1.
  - W(Q) is Zariski-dense (every Pythagorean conic in (m,n) for fixed (X_0,Y_0)).
  - No new global control on rational points.
